  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis early this afternoon depict a \nmid/upper wave shifting northeast over the Desert Southwest as a \nsecondary speed max resides near the Upper Mississippi River Valley. \nShowers and weak storms continue to make their way to the northeast \nthrough portions western and central Kansas (primarily west of I-\n135) within a rich PW axis. These will continue and shift east \nheading into the overnight hours amid broad synoptic ascent from the \napproaching jet and persistent low-level theta-e advection. \nAdditionally, short-term guidance highlights increasing instability \nlater this afternoon and early evening over far south central Kansas \n(generally along/west of K-14). At the least, this will allow for \nlocally heavy rainfall in the strongest cells considering PWs \nprogged at 200% of normal for this time of year, though 20-25 kts of \ndeep-layer shear may allow for some storm organization and perhaps \nsome strong to marginally severe wind gusts prior to sunset. This \nthreat will shift east to include locations primarily along and west \nof I-135 on Friday, though for both today and Friday we are not \nexpecting widespread severe storms. \n\nActivity will continue through Friday night into Saturday as the \nupper wave ejects out of the Rockies and into the Plains. At the \nsame time, a frontal boundary will track out of western Kansas and \npush eastward into central Kansas. Clearing skies ahead of this \nboundary will allow for increasing instability and weak capping \nacross portions of central and south central Kansas, and ~30 kts of \ndeep-layer shear may allow for more strong to severe thunderstorms \nthrough the afternoon and evening hours. The positioning of the mid-\nlevel wave does throw some uncertainty into this setup, however, \nwith much of the global suite suggesting a more progressive track \nand widespread subsidence throughout the region amid increasing \nheights in its wake. This would put a damper on storm development \nSaturday afternoon, though considering the instability in place, it \nwill be hard to rule out a few strong to severe storms at this time.\n\nQuieter weather is forecast Sunday into Monday as the upper wave \ndeparts the region. This break will be short-lived, however, as \nglobal models carry another wave into the region by late Monday \nnight. This will bring more chances for widespread showers and \nstorms to the forecast area with the potential for locally heavy \nrainfall/flooding should trends continue to slow the wave's \nprogression. Stay tuned as additional details come into focus.\n\nIn regards to temperatures, areas under abundant cloud coverage \nshould see seasonable to seasonably cool temperatures (highs in the \nmiddle 70s to near 80) through Friday, while locations that see some \nclearing will warm into the middle 80s. These slightly warmer \ntemperatures will overspread the entire area Saturday through Monday \nas layer thicknesses increase in the wake of Saturday's storms. \nHighs ranging from the middle 70s to near 80 will return Tuesday \nwith the arrival of the next storm system.",
